1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Look at this sealed sparkling drink.

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:04,880
There is liquid below and a space containing

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:05,920
gas above it.

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:07,920
The bottle has been left at the same

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:09,920
temperature long enough to settle.

6
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:13,120
It can look unchanged, even though carbon dioxide

7
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:15,880
is still moving between those two places.

8
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:18,160
Let us look inside and follow what the

9
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:19,280
molecules do.

10
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:23,580
Some carbon dioxide is dissolved in the drink.

11
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:26,580
Dissolved means it is mixed through the liquid,

12
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:28,500
rather than sitting in a visible bubble.

13
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:31,620
A carbon dioxide molecule can leave the liquid

14
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:33,580
and enter the gas above it.

15
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:36,100
A molecule from the gas can also enter

16
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:37,780
the liquid and dissolve.

17
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:39,940
These are opposite processes.

18
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:42,980
The same kind of molecule moves between liquid

19
00:00:43,020 --> 00:00:45,220
and gas; it does not turn into a

20
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:47,220
different substance in this example.

21
00:00:48,434 --> 00:00:51,073
Now watch the amount of carbon dioxide dissolved

22
00:00:51,114 --> 00:00:51,993
in the drink.

23
00:00:52,274 --> 00:00:54,953
A molecule leaving reduces that amount.

24
00:00:55,154 --> 00:00:57,513
A molecule entering adds to it.

25
00:00:57,794 --> 00:01:00,993
At equilibrium, molecules enter just as quickly as

26
00:01:01,074 --> 00:01:02,593
they leave, on average.

27
00:01:03,074 --> 00:01:05,473
So the amount dissolved does not keep rising

28
00:01:05,554 --> 00:01:06,473
or falling.

29
00:01:06,754 --> 00:01:09,313
The gas above the drink stays steady too,

30
00:01:09,474 --> 00:01:12,433
because each place receives carbon dioxide as quickly

31
00:01:12,474 --> 00:01:13,313
as it loses it.

32
00:01:13,874 --> 00:01:15,473
The molecules keep moving.

33
00:01:15,874 --> 00:01:17,873
It is the balance of the two processes

34
00:01:17,954 --> 00:01:19,633
that keeps the amounts steady.

35
00:01:20,834 --> 00:01:23,233
We call this dynamic equilibrium.

36
00:01:23,314 --> 00:01:26,913
Dynamic means the opposing processes continue.

37
00:01:27,474 --> 00:01:30,593
Equilibrium means their effects balance, so there is

38
00:01:30,754 --> 00:01:33,233
no overall change under these conditions.

39
00:01:33,874 --> 00:01:36,073
A rate tells us how much happens in

40
00:01:36,114 --> 00:01:37,073
a given time.

41
00:01:37,634 --> 00:01:40,113
Here, the rate of carbon dioxide leaving the

42
00:01:40,194 --> 00:01:42,353
liquid matches the rate entering it.

43
00:01:42,914 --> 00:01:45,073
Both rates are greater than zero.

44
00:01:45,394 --> 00:01:48,273
Think of two-way traffic: the same rate, but

45
00:01:48,514 --> 00:01:50,513
possibly different amounts on each side.

46
00:01:51,074 --> 00:01:53,953
We are matching movement per second, not sharing

47
00:01:54,034 --> 00:01:56,273
the molecules into two equal piles.

48
00:01:57,500 --> 00:01:59,460
Compare that with a book resting on a

49
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:00,220
table.

50
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:02,780
The table supports the book, and the forces

51
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:03,820
on it balance.

52
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:06,060
The whole book stays at rest.

53
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:09,180
This is static mechanical equilibrium.

54
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:11,580
It does not mean the particles inside the

55
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:13,060
book stop moving.

56
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:17,020
Return to our drink: carbon dioxide continues crossing

57
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:18,540
between liquid and gas.

58
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:22,980
Here the molecules change location, not substance.

59
00:02:23,340 --> 00:02:25,180
In the next lesson, we will apply this

60
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:28,940
idea of continuing opposing processes to a reversible

61
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:32,780
chemical reaction, where reactants become products and products

62
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:34,060
become reactants.

63
00:02:35,267 --> 00:02:38,146
The cap matters because it keeps carbon dioxide

64
00:02:38,387 --> 00:02:39,426
inside the bottle.

65
00:02:39,907 --> 00:02:43,026
We call that a closed system: matter cannot

66
00:02:43,107 --> 00:02:44,506
enter or leave.

67
00:02:44,787 --> 00:02:47,106
Heat can still pass through the bottle, so

68
00:02:47,267 --> 00:02:49,746
closed does not mean insulated.

69
00:02:50,147 --> 00:02:53,066
In our example, the temperature stays constant.

70
00:02:53,427 --> 00:02:54,626
Now open the cap.

71
00:02:55,027 --> 00:02:57,466
Carbon dioxide escapes from the space above the

72
00:02:57,507 --> 00:02:59,506
drink into the much larger room.

73
00:02:59,987 --> 00:03:02,146
There is then less carbon dioxide above the

74
00:03:02,227 --> 00:03:03,826
liquid available to enter it.

75
00:03:04,387 --> 00:03:06,786
The rate returning to the liquid falls below

76
00:03:06,867 --> 00:03:07,826
the rate leaving.

77
00:03:08,387 --> 00:03:10,706
Some gas still dissolves back in, but it

78
00:03:10,787 --> 00:03:12,946
no longer replaces all that leaves.

79
00:03:13,347 --> 00:03:16,706
So the drink loses dissolved carbon dioxide overall.

80
00:03:17,267 --> 00:03:19,826
This is an open system because matter can

81
00:03:19,907 --> 00:03:21,026
cross its boundary.

82
00:03:22,234 --> 00:03:25,113
Try a new record from our sealed-bottle model.

83
00:03:25,354 --> 00:03:28,393
We track only carbon dioxide moving between liquid

84
00:03:28,474 --> 00:03:29,433
and gas.

85
00:03:29,674 --> 00:03:32,873
The temperature and both region volumes stay constant,

86
00:03:32,994 --> 00:03:35,713
and no carbon dioxide enters or leaves the

87
00:03:35,754 --> 00:03:36,353
bottle.

88
00:03:36,634 --> 00:03:39,993
In each of several ten-second intervals, sixty molecules

89
00:03:40,074 --> 00:03:41,153
leave the liquid.

90
00:03:41,434 --> 00:03:43,993
The liquid starts and ends every interval with

91
00:03:44,074 --> 00:03:46,433
one hundred and twenty tracked molecules.

92
00:03:46,714 --> 00:03:48,953
How many molecules must have entered the liquid

93
00:03:49,114 --> 00:03:50,273
in each interval?

94
00:03:50,554 --> 00:03:53,353
Explain your answer, then decide whether this record

95
00:03:53,434 --> 00:03:56,233
shows that exchange has stopped or is continuing

96
00:03:56,314 --> 00:03:57,033
in balance.

97
00:03:57,594 --> 00:03:59,113
Pause if you would like longer.

98
00:04:14,534 --> 00:04:17,733
If sixty molecules left and none came back,

99
00:04:17,774 --> 00:04:19,893
the liquid would lose sixty molecules.

100
00:04:20,374 --> 00:04:22,613
But its tracked amount has not changed.

101
00:04:23,094 --> 00:04:25,653
So sixty must have entered during that same

102
00:04:25,894 --> 00:04:28,573
interval, replacing the sixty that left.

103
00:04:29,014 --> 00:04:31,253
This happens in each interval in the record.

104
00:04:31,814 --> 00:04:34,533
Both directions are continuing, and their average rates

105
00:04:34,694 --> 00:04:38,213
match: sixty molecules in ten seconds each way.

106
00:04:38,774 --> 00:04:42,533
That is balanced exchange, not stopped exchange.

107
00:04:43,014 --> 00:04:45,013
Notice that we worked out the return from

108
00:04:45,054 --> 00:04:46,533
the amount balance.

109
00:04:46,614 --> 00:04:48,853
We did not assume the two places contained

110
00:04:48,934 --> 00:04:50,613
equal numbers of molecules.

111
00:04:51,834 --> 00:04:54,873
So, what still moves in our steady-looking sealed

112
00:04:54,954 --> 00:04:55,353
drink?

113
00:04:55,914 --> 00:04:58,633
Carbon dioxide keeps moving out of the liquid

114
00:04:58,714 --> 00:04:59,953
and back into it.

115
00:05:00,234 --> 00:05:03,513
At dynamic equilibrium, the opposing rates match on

116
00:05:03,594 --> 00:05:07,433
average, keeping the amounts steady under unchanged conditions.

117
00:05:07,914 --> 00:05:10,073
A steady appearance on its own would not

118
00:05:10,154 --> 00:05:11,273
tell us all of that.

119
00:05:11,514 --> 00:05:14,633
Our model shows the continuing exchange that explains

120
00:05:14,714 --> 00:05:14,873
it.

121
00:05:15,434 --> 00:05:18,313
Next, we will follow a reversible chemical reaction

122
00:05:18,394 --> 00:05:21,273
before equilibrium and see how its two rates

123
00:05:21,354 --> 00:05:22,393
come to match.

124
00:05:22,714 --> 00:05:23,953
Here are the key notes.

125
00:05:24,154 --> 00:05:25,833
Pause if you would like time to copy

126
00:05:25,954 --> 00:05:26,233
them.
